You can modify some system defaults on the fly by using control files,
which will affect either some system-wide services, maintenance agents
behaviours and/or all hosted Ægir instances, so these controls belong
to the system root, and are located, if needed, in the root home directory
(/root/) -- except config-style controls now being migrated to /etc/boa/, whose
entries below show that path; secrets and install-critical markers stay in /root/.

These system-level control files use .cnf file extension, so for extra
clarity we will list here also some other .cnf files which either have
special purpose or should never be edited, because they are managed
by the BOA install and upgrade tools exclusively.


 ### The .cnf file to never touch

 @=> /root/.my.cnf

  This is a mysql specific file, which holds your database server
  master (root) password. This file allows you to access your db server
  with root privileges when you are logged in as a system root without
  the need to type mysql root password. It also allows various BOA specific
  maintenance agents to monitor your system status and perform auto-healing,
  databases maintenance, repairs and backups.

  By the way, there is also directly associated /root/.my.pass.txt file
  which includes mysql root password generated. You should never touch
  nor modify this file as well.


 ### Special purpose .cnf files

 @=> /root/.barracuda.cnf

  This is your BOA system master configuration file. Please read its
  template in docs/cnf/barracuda.cnf for more information.

 @=> /root/.USER.octopus.cnf

  This is your Octopus Satellite instance configuration file. If you have
  more Octopus instances, you have a separate file per instance, where
  the USER is a keyword for the instance main system account. Please read
  its template in docs/cnf/octopus.cnf for more information.

 @=> /root/.ip.protected.vhost.whitelist.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to whitelist IP addresses (one valid IP per line)
  for access to vhosts protected via valid shell login (chive, cgp, sqlbuddy).


 ### All other (empty) .cnf control files

 @=> /etc/boa/.mysqladmin.monitor.cnf

  Converted to _MYSQLADMIN_MONITOR=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable
  is the supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. Logs the
  output of `mysqladmin proc` in /var/log/boa/mysqladmin.monitor.log every 15
  seconds. Absence is the ACTIVE state: it turns slow_query_log off on the
  running server and deletes both that monitor log and the slow-query log. To
  switch it off during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the
  file -- while the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

 @=> /root/.fast.cron.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to speed up the Ægir tasks and sites cron queues
  on all hosted Octopus instances, so instead of runing every minute, it will
  run every 10 seconds. Note that while it may be handy during development,
  it will cause higher system load, even with its built-in prevention from
  running more than two concurrent queues, so it is not recommended to use on
  production systems.

 @=> /root/.force.drupalgeddon.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to force running drupalgeddon checks daily
  on all Octopus instances, even if they are not enabled with instance level
  control file ~/static/control/drupalgeddon.info

 @=> /root/.force.sites.verify.cnf

  This file, if exists, will result with all sites hosted on all Octopus
  instances on the same BOA system being re-verified daily. Note that it works
  only if _PERMISSIONS_FIX=YES is set in /root/.barracuda.cnf (default)

 @=> /etc/boa/.enable.newrelic.sysmond.cnf

  Converted to _ENABLE_NEWRELIC_SYSMOND=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the
  variable is the supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release
  while fleets converge. Enables the newrelic-sysmond service, otherwise
  disabled for security reasons, because it exposes too much system level
  information/details in the New Relic control panel. Two enforcement arms:
  the ~5s watchdog (restart when down / stop when disabled) and the
  barracuda upgrade pass itself, which restarts or stops the service with
  the same test whenever /etc/newrelic/nrsysmond.cfg exists.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.php.fpm.reload.cnf

  Converted to _ALLOW_PHP_FPM_RELOAD=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf (the file
  stays honoured for one release). Enables the self-service
  run-php-fpm-reload.pid sentinel box-wide, regardless of the per-account
  plan gate (POWER/PHANTOM/CLUSTER/ULTRA/MONSTER). Note the shared reload
  cooldown: a sentinel dropped inside the cooldown window is discarded, not
  deferred.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow-codebasecheck.cnf

  Converted to _ALLOW_CODEBASECHECK=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf (the file
  stays honoured for one release). Runs codebasecheck once per platform per
  night in the owl maintenance sweep; findings go to the per-account night
  log and, for non-OK codebases, /var/log/boa/core/. System cnf only -- a
  same-named line in an octopus cnf is unsupported.

 @=> /etc/boa/.instant.csf.block.cnf

  Converted to _INSTANT_CSF_BLOCK=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf (the file
  stays honoured for one release). Offenders the web IDS decides to ban
  also get an immediate csf temp ban on ports 80/443, ahead of the
  web.log -> guest-fire pipeline. Requires csf installed; without it the
  toggle has no effect.

 @=> /root/.use.local.nameservers.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to use original (or custom) nameservers provided
  on the system install by your hosting provider.

  It depends on existence of another file with custom name servers to use
  listed, one per line: /var/backups/resolv.conf.vanilla -- for example:

    nameserver 12.34.56.78
    nameserver 12.34.56.00

  The change will take effect on barracuda upgrade.

 @=> /root/.use.default.nameservers.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to revert BOA DNS cache configuration
  to use Unbound server and Cloudflare/Cleaner/Google DNS again (default).

  Note that to restore default DNS cache configuration on barracuda upgrade,
  you must delete the /root/.use.local.nameservers.cnf file, if still exists.

 @=> /etc/boa/.deny.jetty9.cnf

  Converted to _DENY_JETTY9=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Tears down the Jetty 9 service on the next self-update pass: deregisters it
  from the runlevels, parks its init script in /var/backups and kills the
  daemon. The teardown does not reverse itself -- removing the switch later
  does not reinstall Jetty.

 @=> /etc/boa/.deny.solr7.cnf

  Converted to _DENY_SOLR7=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Same teardown for the Solr 7 service, and equally one-way.

 @=> /etc/boa/.deny.solr9.cnf

  Converted to _DENY_SOLR9=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Same teardown for the Solr 9 service, and equally one-way.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.nginx.restart.cnf

  Converted to _ALLOW_NGINX_RESTART=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Enables the self-service run-nginx-restart.pid sentinel for every account on
  the box, beside the qualifying-plan gate rather than replacing it. The
  restart stays cooldown-paced, and a request landing inside a cooldown is
  kept for the next pass.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.valkey.restart.cnf

  Converted to _ALLOW_VALKEY_RESTART=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Enables the self-service cache-restart sentinel for every account on the box,
  beside the qualifying-plan gate. One variable covers both this file and the
  older /etc/boa/.allow.redis.restart.cnf. Note for redis-only boxes: the
  legacy redis watchdog reads only the redis-named file today, so the variable
  opens a gate there that the valkey-named file alone did not.

 @=> /etc/boa/.debug.email.txt

  Converted to _DEBUG_EMAIL=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Sends the daily usage report to the internal debug inbox instead of the
  account owner. The destination is fixed in the code, not configurable.

 @=> /etc/boa/.goaccess.all.cnf

  Converted to _GOACCESS_ALL=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Nightly GoAccess also builds the aggregate ALL-sites report, not just the
  per-site ones. Has no effect unless GoAccess itself is switched on.

 @=> /etc/boa/.silent-octopus-upgrade.cnf

  Converted to _OCTOPUS_UPGRADE_RERUN_SILENT=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Two effects, not one: it suppresses the "already up to date" notice AND
  re-runs an Octopus upgrade that would otherwise be skipped as current.

 @=> /etc/boa/.instant.busy.mysql.action.cnf

  Converted to _INSTANT_BUSY_MYSQL_ACTION=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Adds a second saturation detector to the mysql monitor: besides the syslog
  scan it asks the server directly on every pass and treats a "Too many
  connections" reply as the trigger. It does NOT restart. That reply proves the
  server is answering, and the restart path returns without acting on a server
  that answers, so the marker used to buy nothing at all. It now relieves the
  saturation instead -- flush-hosts and the long-query sweep -- and stands down
  while a Provision task is in flight, as the syslog arm already did.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.sendmail.cnf

  Converted to _ALLOW_SENDMAIL=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Leaves /usr/sbin/sendmail executable for tenants and keeps Postfix in
  direct-delivery mode instead of routing through the configured relay. Both
  directions are re-asserted on every pass, so the switch is self-correcting.

 @=> /root/.debug-purge-build-tree.cnf

  Converted to _DEBUG_PURGE_BUILD_TREE=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Purges the reusable /opt/tmp build tree at the start of every barracuda or
  octopus wrapper run, forcing a full re-download of the sources.

 @=> /root/.debug_daily.info

  Converted to _DEBUG_DAILY=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Adds one verbose line per drush call to the nightly maintenance log, on
  stderr, so it never enters a value the nightly parses from a call's stdout.

 @=> /root/.disable.auditd.logs.cnf

  Converted to _DISABLE_AUDITD_LOGS=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Stops auditd writing logs. Absence is the ACTIVE state: the else arm
  re-asserts write_logs = yes on every barracuda pass, so a hand-edit of
  auditd.conf is reverted the same way.

 @=> /root/.dont.use.fancy.bash.login.cnf

  Converted to _DONT_USE_FANCY_BASH_LOGIN=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Drops the decorated root login banner and the packages behind it (toilet,
  figlet, screenfetch).

 @=> /root/.include-php-latest.cnf

  Converted to _INCLUDE_PHP_LATEST=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file: while
  the file exists it wins and is re-asserted on every pass.

  Includes the newest supported PHP version in multi-version installs.

 @=> /root/.install.myquick.src.info

  RETIRED 2026-08-10: building mydumper from source is an internal fallback
  for OS codenames with no packaged build, not an operator switch. The
  source path is pinned to the older mydumper release, so forcing it on a
  codename that has a package held mydumper a version line back while
  myloader could come from elsewhere, and database imports then failed on
  the command-line option the two binaries no longer agreed about. The
  marker and the _INSTALL_MYQUICK_SRC variable are both ignored; a stale
  marker is inert, and the cnf key is dropped from /root/.barracuda.cnf on
  the next barracuda run.

 @=> /root/.use.sysklogd.cnf

  RETIRED 2026-08-01: rsyslog is the only supported syslog daemon and is
  never removed. The sysklogd arm purged rsyslog first, but sysklogd has no
  install candidate on current Devuan/Debian (verified on the bookworm pool
  and on Devuan testing), so an opted-in box ended up with no syslog daemon
  at all. The OpenVZ inetutils-syslogd arm is retired with it (no OpenVZ
  hosts remain). The marker and the briefly-lived _USE_SYSKLOGD variable are
  both ignored; a stale marker or cnf line is inert, and every barracuda
  pass restores a missing rsyslog - installing it over a still-running
  legacy daemon swaps the old one out via the packages' mutual conflict
  (verified live).

 @=> /root/.deny.java.cnf

  Keeps Java/Solr/Jetty off this box; xmass also sets it at migration
  cutover. Converted to _DENY_JAVA=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf (xmass now
  writes both forms) -- the variable is the supported switch; the file stays
  honoured for one release. To re-enable Solr, set _DENY_JAVA=NO *and* remove
  the file -- then re-arm the service, because autoupboa has by then moved
  /etc/init.d/solr9 to /var/backups/solr9.initd and nothing puts it back
  (_if_solr_nine only reinstalls when the data dir or version stamp is gone).
  On a rolled-back migration source, 'xmass restore-solr' does all of it in
  the required order.

 @=> /etc/boa/.dont.touch.permissions.cnf

  Skips the nightly per-platform permissions pass (the codebase lock); the
  box-wide sweep keeps its own gate. Converted to _SKIP_PERMISSIONS_PASS=YES
  in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the supported switch; the file
  stays honoured for one release; off = NO *and* remove the file.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.aegir.queue.cnf

  Allows the automatic Aegir task queue on a CI-classified box. Converted to
  _ALLOW_AEGIR_QUEUE=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- same one-release
  transition rules as above.

 @=> /etc/boa/.disable_mysql_cleanup.cnf

  Disables the hourly database cleanup run and the four nightly giant-table
  truncations in the backup pass (the two halves deliberately differ in
  scope). Converted to _DISABLE_MYSQL_CLEANUP=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf --
  same one-release transition rules.

 @=> /etc/boa/.debug.monitor.cnf

  Enables the abuse-guard scorer's debug instrumentation including the shell
  trace on manual runs. Converted to _DEBUG_MONITOR=YES in
  /root/.barracuda.cnf -- same one-release transition rules.

 @=> /etc/boa/.debug.monitor.log.cnf

  FOLDED into the existing _NGINX_DOS_LOG key: while the file exists the
  upgrade pass ensures _NGINX_DOS_LOG=NORMAL (an explicit NORMAL or VERBOSE
  in the cnf wins). Off = remove the file *and* set _NGINX_DOS_LOG=SILENT.

 @=> /etc/boa/.allow.du.cnf

  Keeps du available in the restricted client shell. Converted to
  _ALLOW_DU=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- same one-release transition rules.

 @=> /root/.allow.clamav.cnf
 @=> /root/.deny.clamav.cnf

  The pair governs ClamAV service registration; absence of allow already
  denies, deny overrides an allow. Converted to _ALLOW_CLAMAV / _DENY_CLAMAV
  in /root/.barracuda.cnf (the pair converts together; every read site
  honours both forms). Until the next release serial reaches the two
  slow-cycle OS-upgrade drivers, pair _ALLOW_CLAMAV=YES with the marker file.

 @=> /etc/boa/.look.like.jenkins.cnf

  Forces the CI box classification (queue policy, monitor cadence, usage
  accounting, the Jenkins-era java symlink set); real Jenkins presence is
  auto-detected separately. Converted to _FORCE_CI_BOX=YES in
  /root/.barracuda.cnf -- same one-release transition rules.

 @=> /root/.big_db.cnf
 @=> /root/.high_load.cnf

  RETIRED as inert: the cnf SQL TTL value is authoritative on every box (the
  watchdog sources the cnf before its fallback can fire), so the historical
  marker-driven retune never had an observable effect and was removed. Set
  the SQL TTL key explicitly if a custom ceiling is intended; the upgrade
  pass leaves a one-time advisory note when either file is present.

 @=> /root/.hr.monitor.cnf

  RETIRED. This file no longer does anything: its last reader went with the
  legacy Perl monitors, and BOA no longer writes or removes it. The knob it
  was believed to set is _NGINX_DOS_MODE in /root/.barracuda.cnf (default 2;
  set it to 1 for the more aggressive abuse-guard mode). An existing file is
  harmless and can be deleted at leisure.

 @=> /root/.no.fpm.cpu.limit.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to disable aggressive php-fpm processes
  monitoring and killing if any is using really too much CPU power.

 @=> /root/.no.sql.cpu.limit.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to disable mysql processes monitoring
  and restarting mysql server if any is using really too much CPU power.

 @=> /root/.no.swap.clear.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to skip the otherwise default procedure
  designed to clear system (memory) swap daily.

 @=> /root/.no.sysctl.update.cnf

  Converted to _NO_SYSCTL_UPDATE=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable
  is the supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release. Skips the
  /etc/sysctl.conf and system-limits refresh that otherwise runs on every
  barracuda upgrade. To switch it off during the transition, set the variable
  to NO *and* remove the file -- while the file exists it wins.

 @=> /root/.mysql.yes.new.password.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to automatically generate random, new mysql
  root password on every barracuda upgrade. This file is ignored if
  /root/.mysql.no.new.password.cnf file also exists, but is enabled by default.

 @=> /root/.mysql.no.new.password.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to ignore /root/.mysql.yes.new.password.cnf
  as mentioned above.

 @=> /root/.mysql.force.legacy.backup.cnf

  This file, if exists, will ignore the default mysql backup mode with separate
  SQL dump files per table, and will force the legacy single-file database
  mysqldump mode for automatic daily backups of all databases, stored
  in the /data/disk/arch/sql/ directory. It will not affect any Octopus
  instance configuration with ~/static/control/MyQuick.info file present,
  since it's used only by Ægir tasks.

 @=> /root/.valkey.no.new.password.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to skip the otherwise default procedure
  designed to generate new Valkey password on every barracuda upgrade.

 @=> /root/.redis.no.new.password.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to skip the otherwise default procedure
  designed to generate new Redis password on every barracuda upgrade.

 @=> /root/.allow.mc.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to open an access for all limited shell users
  to the Midnight Commander (mc) -- a file manager available on command
  line, a Unix clone of Norton Commander known in the ancient DOS days.

  While very useful for novice users, can affect your system access separation
  because it doesn't respect built-in virtual chroot/jail enforced normally
  both in the limited shell and in SFTP via MySecureShell. Do not use it,
  unless you don't open shell access for untrusted users.

 @=> /root/.high_traffic.cnf

  Recommended if you have a very busy site(s) hosted. It prevents PHP-FPM
  restarts when the system detects segfault. Note that unlike before,
  even without this file, PHP-FPM will not be restarted at midnight,
  thanks to some improvements elsewhere in the self-healing procedures.

 @=> /root/.giant_traffic.cnf

  Recommended if you have an extremely busy site(s) hosted. It prevents
  the Speed Booster (Nginx) cache cleanup (for entries older than 1 day),
  which happens hourly. However, even with very busy sites hosted, it will be
  very rarely needed, since the cleanup procedure has been improved
  to not cause load spikes, even under "Giant Traffic" pressure.

 @=> /root/.skip_cleanup.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to skip all daily cleanup otherwise run via
  /var/xdrago/graceful.sh -- it works like /root/.giant_traffic.cnf and
  additionally disables /tmp/ and /opt/tmp/ cleanup and Jetty restart.

 @=> /root/.skip_duplicity_monthly_cleanup.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to skip forced duplicity backup cleanup,
  which is otherwise run at the beginning (randomly 1-5) of each month.

 @=> /root/.randomize_duplicity_full_backup_day.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to randomize duplicity full backup schedule,
  which is otherwise set to run on Sunday for backboa and Saturday for
  duobackboa. The full backup day (Mon-Sun) will be randomized monthly or
  one-time if /root/.skip_duplicity_monthly_cleanup.cnf file exists.
  This feature is useful when you have many VM/BOA instances running
  on the same machine.

 @=> /etc/boa/.home.no.wildcard.chmod.cnf

  This file, if exists, allows to avoid setting restrictive (but recommended)
  permissions on all directories in the /home/* directory tree, where normally
  non-system users have their account home directories. Converted to
  _HOME_NO_WILDCARD_CHMOD=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the
  supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release; off = NO *and*
  remove the file.

  Without this file the system will run 'chmod 700 /home/*' every 5 minutes.

 @=> /root/.my.batch_innodb.cnf

  This file, if exists, will enable non-standard procedure during nightly
  (global) databases backup, which normally starts at 3:01 AM.

  This special procedure will be run only once per week, on Saturday.

  It will run a three-step sequence on every database: repair/rebuild all
  tables, truncate all cache* tables, plus convert all tables to InnoDB,
  which in turn runs also optimize.

 @=> /root/.my.optimize.cnf

  This file, if exists, will enable non-standard procedure during nightly
  (global) databases backup, which normally starts at 3:01 AM.

  This special procedure will be run only once per month, on the last Sunday.

  It will run a three-step sequence on every database: repair/rebuild all
  tables, truncate all cache* tables, and optimize all tables.

  While it usually allows to defragment and shrink the binary space used
  by database tables, it may easily cause serious I/O load and subsequent
  system load (and slowdown) if you happen to host many sites on a weak
  VPS or machine with slow disks or not fast enough CPU etc.

  Note: this is a system-wide setting.

  There is a similar option: _SQL_CONVERT (defaults to NO), available in every
  Octopus instance /root/.USER.octopus.cnf file, which has very similar purpose,
  because it will trigger automatic 'to-innodb' or 'to-myisam' smart conversion
  performed via 'sqlmagic' tool, and since it uses the 'ALTER TABLE' command,
  in turn it performs auto-optimization on the fly.

  This optional conversion starts every Saturday at 3:01 AM and runs until
  the agent completes all tasks included in the /var/xdrago/daily.sh script.

  If _SQL_CONVERT=NO is set, the conversion mode can be individually enabled
  and more precisely configured with variable:

    sql_conversion_mode

  if set in the site and/or platform level, active INI files:

    boa_platform_control.ini
    boa_site_control.ini

  More info: https://docs.boa.io/operating/control-files-ini/ini-precedence

  Please note that if you will change it to _SQL_CONVERT=YES, the system will
  ignore sql_conversion_mode variables set in the active INI files, and instead
  will force conversion to InnoDB format in all sites hosted on this instance.

  By the way, conversion to MyISAM format will still keep some tables at InnoDB,
  and exceptions are defined with regex:

  (cache_[a-z_]+|cache|sessions|users|watchdog|accesslog)

 @=> /root/.my.restart_after_optimize.cnf

  Converted to _MY_RESTART_AFTER_OPTIMIZE=YES in /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the
  variable is the supported switch; the file stays honoured for one release.
  Triggers a graceful database restart once all tables in all databases are
  optimized, and is still ignored unless the monthly OPTIMIZE pass itself runs
  (it keeps its Sunday, day-of-month and full-mode gates). To switch it off
  during the transition, set the variable to NO *and* remove the file.

 @=> /root/.upstart.cnf

  Suppresses the automatic cron service start/reload at the three sites that
  honour it -- two upgrade-finale starts and one per-tick reload (the
  historical stop-skipping half no
  longer exists in code; the name is historical too -- it neither detects nor
  selects an init system). Converted to _NO_CRON_AUTOSTART=YES in
  /root/.barracuda.cnf -- the variable is the supported switch; the file stays
  honoured for one release. To switch it off during the transition, set the
  variable to NO *and* remove the file.
